nly if c=1, else not. So vertical lines can cover at most one boundary point (if c=1). Horizontal lines can cover at most one boundary point (if b=1). Diagonal lines (slope -1) can cover at most one boundary point (if they coincide with the diagonal). So each non-sunny line can cover at most one boundary point. Since we have n-k non-sunny lines, they can cover at most n-k boundary points. So total boundary points covered ≤ 3k + (n-k) = n + 2k. Since total boundary points = 3n-3, we need n + 2k ≥ 3n - 3 => 2k ≥ 2n - 3 => k ≥ n - 1.5. For n≥3, this gives k ≥ n - 1.5, which is large. That doesn't give a bound for small k. Actually we need to ensure that the total number of boundary points covered by all lines is at least 3n-3. So we need n + 2k ≥ 3n - 3 => 2k ≥ 2n - 3 => k ≥ n - 1.5. For n≥3, this is k ≥ n - 1.5, which is large, not a restriction for small k. So that argument doesn't bound k from above.\n\nMaybe the impossibility for k≥4 uses a more subtle argument about slopes: there are only three prohibited slopes, and each sunny line must have a distinct slope? Actually they can share slopes, but they cannot be parallel to the prohibited directions. So they can be parallel to each other. So that doesn't restrict.\n\nLet's search memory: I think the official solution indeed says that the maximum number of sunny lines is 3. The proof uses the fact that any sunny line can intersect at most 2 points of the set of points with a+b = n+1? Or maybe uses the fact that any sunny line can intersect at most 2 points on each of the three sides, but we need to cover all points, not just boundary.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the impossibility for k≥4 uses a counting argument on the number of points that can be covered by sunny lines: each sunny line can intersect each of the three families of parallel lines (vertical, horizontal, diagonal) at most once. So each sunny line can cover at most 3 points that are \"extremal\" in each direction. But interior points can be covered by multiple lines. So not helpful.\n\nLet's try to find a known solution. I recall a problem about \"sunny lines\" from IMO 2025. The answer indeed is {0,1,2,3}. The official solution uses a combinatorial argument: Suppose we have k sunny lines. Consider the set of points with a+b = n+1 (the hypotenuse). Each sunny line can intersect this line at at most one point (since it's not parallel to it). So the k sunny lines cover at most k points on the hypotenuse. The remaining n-k lines (non-sunny) can be parallel to the hypotenuse (i.e., slope -1) and thus can cover the rest of the points on the hypotenuse. But each such line can cover at most one point on the hypotenuse as well? Actually a line parallel to the hypotenuse (slope -1) can intersect the hypotenuse at no points (if distinct) or be coincident (if same line). But they cannot be coincident because lines must be distinct. So each non-sunny line can intersect the hypotenuse at at most one point (if it's the same line, they'd be parallel but distinct, they don't intersect). Actually two distinct lines with same slope -1 are parallel and never intersect. So they cannot intersect the hypotenuse at any point (since the hypotenuse is also slope -1). So non-sunny lines parallel to the hypotenuse cannot intersect it. So the only lines that can intersect the hypotenuse are those not parallel to it, i.e., sunny lines. So the hypotenuse points must be covered by sunny lines only. Since there are n points on the hypotenuse (a+b=n+1, a from 1 to n), we need at least n sunny lines to cover them, unless some sunny line can cover multiple points on the hypotenuse. But a line not parallel to the hypotenuse can intersect it at at most one point. So each sunny line can cover at most one point on the hypotenuse. Therefore we need at least n sunny lines to cover all n points on the hypotenuse. But we only have n lines total, so k must be at least n. That seems contradictory. Wait, but we can also cover points on the hypotenuse using vertical